::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A right triangle has legs 6 and 8. What is the hypotenuse?
{
=A. 10#Correct. 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100, and sqrt(100) = 10.
~B. 14#This adds the legs. Use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~C. 48#This multiplies the legs. Multiplication alone does not find the hypotenuse.
~D. 100#This is c², not c. Take the square root.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A right triangle has legs 9 and 12. What is the hypotenuse?
{
~A. 21#This adds the legs. The Pythagorean Theorem uses squares.
~B. 108#This multiplies the legs. Multiplication alone does not find the hypotenuse.
=C. 15#Correct. The hypotenuse is sqrt(225), which equals 15.
~D. 225#This is c², not the side length.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Which side is always across from the right angle?
{
~A. adjacent side#Adjacent depends on the acute angle being used, not the right angle.
=B. hypotenuse#Correct. The hypotenuse is across from the right angle.
~C. opposite side#Opposite depends on the acute angle being used.
~D. shorter leg#A shorter leg touches the right angle, not across from it.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Which equation represents the Pythagorean Theorem?
{
~A. a + b = c#This adds sides and does not square them.
~B. ab = c#This multiplies two sides, which is not the theorem.
~C. a² - b² = c²#The theorem adds the squares of the legs.
=D. a² + b² = c²#Correct. The Pythagorean Theorem is a² + b² = c².
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Which ratio compares adjacent to hypotenuse?
{
~A. sine#Sine compares opposite to hypotenuse.
=B. cosine#Correct. Cosine compares adjacent to hypotenuse.
~C. tangent#Tangent compares opposite to adjacent.
~D. area#Area is not a trigonometric ratio.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A right triangle has legs 8 and 15. What is the hypotenuse?
{
=A. 17#Correct. 8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 289, and sqrt(289) = 17.
~B. 23#This adds the legs. Use squares and a square root.
~C. 120#This multiplies the legs. That does not find the hypotenuse.
~D. 289#This is c², not c.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A ladder is 13 feet long and reaches 12 feet up a wall. How far is the base from the wall?
{
~A. 25 feet#This adds 13 and 12. Use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~B. 1 foot#This subtracts 12 from 13, which is not enough for this right-triangle model.
~C. 169 feet#This uses the hypotenuse squared as the answer.
=D. 5 feet#Correct. a² + 12² = 13², so a² = 25 and a = 5.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Which ratio compares opposite to adjacent?
{
~A. sine#Sine compares opposite to hypotenuse.
~B. cosine#Cosine compares adjacent to hypotenuse.
=C. tangent#Correct. Tangent compares opposite to adjacent.
~D. volume#Volume is not a trigonometric ratio.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A right triangle has legs 7 and 24. What is the hypotenuse?
{
=A. 25#Correct. 7² + 24² = 49 + 576 = 625, and sqrt(625) = 25.
~B. 31#This adds the legs. Use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~C. 168#This multiplies the legs. Multiplication alone does not find c.
~D. 625#This is c², not c.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
If a right triangle has hypotenuse 10 and one leg 6, what is the other leg?
{
~A. 4#This subtracts 6 from 10. Use squared side lengths.
=B. 8#Correct. a² + 6² = 10², so a² = 64 and a = 8.
~C. 16#This adds the known side lengths.
~D. 64#This is a², not the side length.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Which side is the longest side of a right triangle?
{
~A. either leg#The legs form the right angle and are shorter than the hypotenuse.
~B. opposite side#Opposite depends on the angle being used.
=C. hypotenuse#Correct. The hypotenuse is always the longest side of a right triangle.
~D. adjacent side#Adjacent depends on the angle being used and is not always longest.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Which ratio should be used when a problem connects opposite and hypotenuse?
{
=A. sine#Correct. Sine compares opposite to hypotenuse.
~B. cosine#Cosine compares adjacent to hypotenuse.
~C. tangent#Tangent compares opposite to adjacent.
~D. perimeter#Perimeter is not a trigonometric ratio.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A right triangle has legs 12 and 16. What is the hypotenuse?
{
~A. 28#This adds the legs. Use the Pythagorean Theorem.
~B. 400#This is c², not the side length c.
~C. 192#This multiplies the legs. Multiplication alone does not find the hypotenuse.
=D. 20#Correct. The hypotenuse is sqrt(400), which equals 20.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
If a right triangle has hypotenuse 17 and one leg 8, what is the other leg?
{
~A. 9#This subtracts 8 from 17. Use the Pythagorean Theorem.
=B. 15#Correct. a² + 8² = 17², so a² = 225 and a = 15.
~C. 25#This adds 17 and 8, which is not a missing side.
~D. 225#This is a², not the side length.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Why is a diagram useful in a right-triangle modeling problem?
{
~A. It replaces all calculations#A diagram supports calculations but does not replace them.
~B. It guarantees the answer is whole#Right-triangle answers are not always whole numbers.
=C. It helps label legs, hypotenuse, and angle relationships#Correct. Labels help choose the correct theorem or ratio.
~D. It changes the measurements#A diagram organizes measurements but does not change them.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A support cable is 25 feet long and reaches 24 feet up a pole. How far is the cable anchor from the pole?
{
~A. 49 feet#This adds 25 and 24. Use squared side lengths.
~B. 1 foot#This subtracts 24 from 25. That is not the right-triangle distance.
~C. 625 feet#This uses the hypotenuse squared as the answer.
=D. 7 feet#Correct. a² + 24² = 25², so a² = 49 and a = 7.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Which side does c represent in a² + b² = c²?
{
=A. hypotenuse#Correct. c represents the hypotenuse in the Pythagorean Theorem.
~B. shorter leg#The shorter leg may be a or b, not c.
~C. longer leg#A leg is not c. c is across from the right angle.
~D. perimeter#c is a side length, not the total distance around the triangle.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
A right triangle has legs 10 and 24. What is the hypotenuse?
{
~A. 34#This adds the legs. Use the Pythagorean Theorem.
=B. 26#Correct. 10² + 24² = 100 + 576 = 676, and sqrt(676) = 26.
~C. 240#This multiplies the legs. Multiplication alone does not find c.
~D. 676#This is c², not c.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
Which ratio should be used when a problem connects adjacent and hypotenuse?
{
~A. sine#Sine uses opposite and hypotenuse.
~B. tangent#Tangent uses opposite and adjacent.
~C. secant#This is not one of the three basic ratios used in this lesson. Use cosine for adjacent and hypotenuse.
=D. cosine#Correct. Cosine compares adjacent to hypotenuse.
}


::MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L06_Quiz_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.7
Question:
If a right triangle has hypotenuse 15 and one leg 9, what is the other leg?
{
~A. 6#This subtracts 9 from 15. Use squared values.
~B. 24#This adds the known side lengths.
=C. 12#Correct. a² + 9² = 15², so a² = 144 and a = 12.
~D. 144#This is a², not the side length.
}

